  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Storm diminish overnight.\n\nA few heavy/strong thunderstorms remain as of about 9:30 this\nevening, mainly in the DC/Baltimore metro (NW suburbs of DC in\nparticular), as well as near Scottsville to Lovingston VA. A\ncouple of other weakening storms near BWI Airport and in western\nMD (approaching Oakland in Garrett County) are also being\nmonitored as they slowly lose steam.\n\nFor the most part, the severe/flood threats heave diminished.\nHowever, isolated downbursts and localized heavy rainfall remain\npossible around DC, as well as in Nelson County VA as these\nstorms are the most potent and have the highest instability\nleftover to work with.\n\nOtherwise, low clouds and patchy fog will likely develop by\nFriday morning especially west of I-95 in areas that got a\nsoaking rain earlier.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions this weekend.\n\nA cold front pushes through the area early Saturday morning, likely \nstalling just south across southern VA to the Carolinas this \nweekend. Surface high pressure builds south into the Mid-Atlantic as \nwinds shift quickly from north to easterly on Saturday. The high \nmoves offshore Sunday, bringing the return of southerly winds. While \nwe remain under broad troughing aloft, the presence of drier air at \nthe surface will keep rain chances out of the forecast. Lingering \nhumidity and building heat make for a warm and muggy weekend as \nhighs reach the 80s to around 90F.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Increasingly hot and humid conditions through\nnext week.\n\nModels are generally in good agreement that a strong mid-level ridge \nbuilds across the southern U.S. and into the Mid-Atlantic during the \nearly to middle part of next week. This brings us well above normal \ntemperatures for late August as highs reach the low to mid 90s. Hot \nand humid conditions are likely as heat indices reach to the low \n100s east of the Blue Ridge. \n\nA series of mid-level disturbances will ride along the top of the \nridge from the northern High Plains to the Northeast next week. \nDepending on the exact position of the ridge, some of these \ndisturbances could be near or over our area come the middle to late \npart of the week. For now, Monday and Tuesday look mostly dry, \nthough cannot rule out a stray thunderstorm or two given the \nhumid/unstable atmosphere. By Wednesday, the upper ridge begins to \nretreat slightly, allowing the shortwave troughs to move over our \narea. Several of the AI models indicate increasing severe potential \nat some point Wednesday to Friday of next week, which is certainly \npossible given the high instability that will be in place, along \nwith substantial upper-level support from any strong disturbance.",
